## 1. Scope

This test strategy governs Issue AD-488, which delivers an authenticated avatar upload and crop experience for public profile identity display. The solution scope is not limited to a single user interface interaction; it encompasses a chained transaction across browser gating, API Gateway admission control, server-side validation, synchronous malware inspection, cloud storage persistence, CDN invalidation, and real-time profile synchronization. The business value of the story depends on a secure and visibly immediate outcome: a user must be able to update a recognizable profile avatar, while the platform must simultaneously prevent unauthorized access, reject malformed or dangerous content, and maintain a consistent avatar state across active views. Because the story includes a public-display identity artifact, release quality must be measured not only by upload success but by correct propagation, correctness of security controls, and reliability of post-upload visibility.

From a quality engineering perspective, the boundaries supplied by the upstream pipeline are complete and internally consistent. Output1 defines the immutable scope boundaries: authenticated users only, server-side-only processing, strict support for JPEG and WebP, 5MB maximum payload with exact 5.00MB acceptance and 5.01MB rejection, synchronous malware scanning before persistence, cloud-bucket storage in an authorized ecosystem, immediate CDN cache invalidation, and dynamic DOM refresh without full browser reload. Output2 preserves those same boundaries in executable form through testing levels, test design techniques, environment needs, and end-to-end approach sequencing. No material contradiction was identified between the business scope and the approved test design. Both prior outputs also preserve the explicit out-of-scope exclusions: no client-side compression, no local scaling or canvas manipulation, no unsupported formats such as PNG/SVG/GIF, no concurrent batch uploads, no multi-image flows, and no external storage ecosystems.

The strategic scope therefore includes validation of authenticated avatar upload flow, crop behavior within the approved story context, cookie-session based UI gating, API Gateway routing and rejection handling, file size boundary enforcement, MIME and magic-number inspection, malware scan ordering, storage placement, CDN purge execution, UI state synchronization, and 2.0-second end-to-end SLA behavior under standard 4G network conditions. The scope also explicitly absorbs two residual review gaps identified in self-healing analysis: proving that the browser transfers raw binary content without client-side compression or canvas/image processing side paths, and proving that traffic traverses the dedicated API Gateway route with observable filtering and policy enforcement. These two gaps are now treated as required validation objectives in this strategy so that release confidence reflects the true implementation architecture rather than a reduced functional-only interpretation.

## 2. Test Management

Test management for AD-488 will operate through a controlled lifecycle spanning requirement intake, test design review, automation development, execution, triage, release recommendation, and post-deployment evidence retention. The issue record AD-488 is the master planning anchor in Jira, and every test artifact produced for this story will link back to that issue key for traceability. Functional scenarios, negative-path scenarios, security validations, and non-functional timing checks will be decomposed into test conditions and then into atomic test cases. Manual exploratory charters will be used during early validation of edge behaviors such as spoofed file signatures, malware-positive handling, and synchronization timing, while stable regression objectives will be automated into repeatable suites. Test case peer review is mandatory before execution promotion into the CI/CD path. No test case will be accepted into the managed suite unless it names the mapped requirement, expected result, dependency set, environment assumptions, and evidence mechanism.

Operational governance follows a gated release model. Human-in-the-Loop Gate 2 was already identified in the upstream approach and remains a mandatory architectural approval point before large-scale automation build-out. For downstream execution, Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 is the release-readiness pause state before production recommendation. At Gate 3, the execution state transitions to Paused until manual validation confirms that all critical-path controls have passed: authenticated access gating, 401 rejection behavior, exact 5MB boundary handling, magic-number enforcement, malware-before-storage sequencing, CDN invalidation evidence, dynamic no-reload UI propagation, and SLA compliance. This pause state protects the release from being mechanically promoted based only on test count completion. The strategy requires explicit human review of risk burndown, defect aging, environment anomalies, and telemetry reliability before resuming toward sign-off.

CI/CD integration will enforce branch-quality controls. Pull requests affecting avatar upload UI, gateway policy, validation microservices, storage routing, or CDN purge logic must trigger at minimum: unit checks, API contract tests, Playwright smoke validation for access gating, PyTest-based service validations, and static evidence publication into the build pipeline. Pull requests may not be merged without at least one peer review from engineering and one QA approval when impacted files touch authentication, file validation, malware handling, or infrastructure policy definitions. Build pipelines must publish artifact links to logs, traces, screenshots, timing metrics, and gateway telemetry so that QA and release management can review execution provenance rather than inferred pass/fail states. This management model ensures that the test strategy is operationally enforceable, not merely descriptive.

## 3. Quality Objectives

The primary quality objective is to ensure that the avatar ingestion capability behaves as a secure, deterministic, and user-visible identity update mechanism under realistic operating conditions. In this story, quality is defined by the conjunction of correctness, security, timing, and propagation. A technically successful upload that bypasses malware scanning, accepts spoofed files, stores in the wrong bucket path, or delays CDN invalidation would still represent a release failure. Therefore, the quality target is not a narrow API success rate; it is end-to-end integrity from authenticated entry point through public presentation state. This objective requires observable evidence that every control in the path executes in the correct sequence and that no protected stage is silently skipped under error or latency pressure.

A second objective is prevention quality. The system must reliably reject invalid states before they become persistence or user-experience defects. That means unauthenticated and expired sessions must never expose actionable upload controls, oversize payloads must be rejected at the gateway boundary, renamed non-image files must fail header validation, and malware-positive files must be blocked before storage commitment or CDN visibility. These are not secondary negative tests; they are central quality objectives because the business promise of safe profile identity display depends on resilient rejection behavior. The strategy therefore prioritizes negative-path depth equal to positive-path verification and treats validation bypasses as high-severity release blockers.

A third objective is operational confidence in user-perceived responsiveness. The accepted business requirement sets a 2.0-second SLA under standard 4G conditions for the complete transaction path through upload transport, malware scan, storage, and public profile edge distribution. This means performance quality cannot be isolated to one API response; it must include distributed timing across gateway, scanner, storage, purge, and UI synchronization layers. The strategy will collect timestamps from observability sources and correlate them with browser-visible updates. Only when the browser state confirms dynamic DOM refresh without full reload, active views show the new avatar, and timing remains inside the SLA envelope will the quality objective be considered satisfied.

### 3.2 Test Scope

#### 3.2.1 In Scope
- Authenticated avatar upload flow
- Avatar crop capability within the approved story path
- Authentication token gating for avatar UI visibility and interactivity
- API Gateway request routing, filtering behavior, and policy observability
- Gateway rejection handling for unauthorized and expired-session requests
- File size boundary validation including below limit, exact 5.00MB, and above limit 5.01MB
- JPEG and WebP format validation
- Magic number and header-byte inspection
- Synchronous malware scanning before persistence
- Verification that browser transfers raw image binary without client-side compression, scaling, or canvas processing
- Server-side processing pipeline behavior
- Cloud bucket storage path validation for accepted images
- CDN edge cache invalidation after successful upload
- Immediate avatar synchronization across active user views and header bars
- Dynamic DOM-based UI update behavior without full page reload
- End-to-end SLA validation under standard 4G network throttling
- Single-avatar upload transaction handling

#### 3.2.2 Out of Scope
- Client-side compression features as product functionality, because the approved architecture explicitly forbids client-side image processing; only absence-of-processing verification is in scope.
- Local image scaling engines and browser canvas editing implementations outside the approved crop flow, because the story excludes custom client-side manipulation.
- Processing of PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design file formats, because acceptance criteria whitelist only JPEG and WebP.
- Concurrent batch uploads, because the functional boundary permits one avatar per request only.
- Multi-image profile header adjustments, because the user story addresses a single public avatar artifact.
- Storage infrastructure or deployments outside the authorized cloud ecosystem, because the approved design restricts persistence targets to authorized cloud resources.
- Broader account profile editing features unrelated to avatar upload, because they are outside the business objective and would dilute release focus.

## 4. Test Approach

The approved approach is tiered across Component/UI, API/Gateway, Service/Integration, Storage/CDN Integration, and End-to-End validation. At the Component/UI level, Playwright-based browser automation and targeted manual reviews will verify visibility states, disabled controls, crop interaction entry conditions, network request initiation, DOM mutation behavior, and absence of full-page reload. Browser instrumentation and network interception will be used to compare uploaded binary characteristics against fixture baselines to support proof that the client is not silently compressing or transforming the image before transfer. This is particularly important because the architecture explicitly bans browser-side manipulation and because prior review identified this as a coverage gap.

At the API/Gateway layer, tests will confirm that requests traverse the dedicated gateway route, that gateway filtering rules are applied, and that policy telemetry records the request path and outcome. PyTest service harnesses and API assertions will validate 200 success handling for valid requests and 401 rejection behavior for missing or expired sessions. Boundary value and robust boundary techniques will be applied to file sizes just below 5MB, exactly 5.00MB, and 5.01MB-plus payloads. Decision tables will cover combinations of session validity, MIME declaration, magic-number validity, malware result, and expected acceptance or rejection outcome. These validations ensure that gateway routing is tested not merely as a pass-through, but as an accountable control plane.

At the service and integration layers, the sequence from gateway to validation service to malware scanner to storage bucket to CDN purge must be traceable and provable. PyTest integration suites will inspect logs, tracing spans, and service responses to confirm that no persistence happens before malware scan completion and that no purge happens before successful storage commitment. At the Storage/CDN layer, telemetry and object metadata will be correlated to prove that accepted files are stored in the correct cloud folder path and that edge invalidation is triggered immediately enough to support the synchronization SLA. At the End-to-End level, JMeter and browser automation under tcconfig-driven network shaping will simulate standard 4G conditions so that the total path can be measured from upload initiation through browser-visible profile refresh. The approach remains tightly aligned to the upstream outputs and directly addresses all approved acceptance criteria.

#### 4.1.1 Test Types
*Describe all the test types in this project and provide the general testing timeline.*

For this project, the active test types are Functional / Integration Testing, Automation Testing, Performance Testing, Security Testing, Journey Testing, IT E2E Testing, Regression Testing, and UAT / Ops Testing. Functional testing validates authenticated upload, crop flow entry, file-size boundaries, accepted formats, storage placement, and UI update behavior. Negative and validation-oriented functional checks also cover spoofed extensions, malformed content, rejected sessions, and oversize gateway drops. Integration testing verifies ordered service execution across gateway, validation, malware scanning, storage, CDN invalidation, and dynamic UI synchronization. These tests begin as soon as the integrated lower environment is available and continue through QA and pre-production regression windows.

Security testing focuses on access gating, token expiry handling, magic-number inspection, malware interception, and upload rejection evidence. Performance testing measures the complete business transaction under standard 4G throttling and validates SLA conformance with representative payloads at realistic boundaries. Journey and IT E2E testing validate that the user-visible profile identity change propagates immediately across active views and header bars without a page reload. Regression testing protects adjacent changes to profile presentation and upload services. UAT / Ops testing confirms operational readiness, observability, and supportability of the live flow. The expected timeline is: early component and API checks during feature development; integrated functional, security, and negative-path testing in QA; performance, storage/CDN, and end-to-end synchronization validation in pre-production; and smoke plus operational verification before release approval.

