### 4.2 Requirement Traceability Matrix
Requirements and user stories will be traced dynamically through Jira issue AD-488, linked test cases, automation identifiers, pull-request references, and execution evidence. Each structured requirement from the approved scope is assigned one or more test objectives and at least one validation method spanning UI, API, integration, or end-to-end coverage. The traceability model is bidirectional: requirements must show linked tests, and every test must identify the source requirement or control objective it proves. This approach prevents orphan automation, reduces duplication, and enables release review to determine not only how many tests passed but which business and technical controls were actually satisfied.

Operationally, requirement traceability is maintained through a versioned mapping sheet stored with the test assets in the source repository and referenced in Jira. Pull requests adding or changing application behavior must update the traceability mapping when they affect authentication, validation, malware scanning, storage, CDN purge, or synchronization logic. During peer review, QA verifies that every changed acceptance criterion has updated test references and that any new failure mode has at least one negative-path case. CI/CD pipeline steps publish test run identifiers back to Jira comments or linked build artifacts so that auditors and release managers can traverse from requirement to commit to pipeline to evidence. This ensures traceability remains a living release control rather than a static spreadsheet artifact.

