### 4.3 Test Control

#### 4.3.1 Defect Tracking
Defect tracking for AD-488 will be managed in Jira using a workflow that reflects both engineering urgency and release-control discipline. Every failed test that indicates a product defect, environment defect, data defect, or observability gap will be logged against the issue context with a unique defect ID, severity, priority, reproducibility status, environment, build number, evidence set, and suspected component owner. Severity definitions are release-oriented: Critical applies to vulnerabilities or failures that compromise security, persistence sequencing, or production identity display integrity; High applies to acceptance-criterion failures such as invalid file acceptance, 5.01MB boundary errors, missing CDN invalidation, or SLA non-compliance; Medium applies to non-blocking UI inconsistencies or observability weaknesses; Low applies to cosmetic or documentation issues. No defect may be advanced to engineering without reproducible steps, expected-versus-actual outcomes, and attached evidence.

The Jira workflow states are Open, Triaged, In Progress, Ready for QA Retest, Retest Failed, Verified, Deferred, and Closed. Daily triage during active execution will review new defects, aging defects, and blocker status. Pull requests fixing defects must reference the Jira defect key, include root-cause notes, and identify whether new automation or regression assertions were added to prevent recurrence. QA retest cannot close a defect based on developer confirmation alone; it requires rerun evidence in the impacted environment and, when relevant, corroborating logs from gateway, validation, malware scan, storage, or CDN layers. For high-severity defects touching authentication, malware scanning, or persistence ordering, peer review from both engineering and QA leads is mandatory before status moves to Verified. This strict discipline ensures defect closure is evidence-driven and aligned with release risk posture.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
Test materials are stored in the source control repository under versioned folders for strategy, traceability mappings, automated test suites, test data manifests, environment configuration references, and execution evidence indexes. The current published strategy is stored at docs/test-strategies/TestStrategy.doc to satisfy downstream document-opening requirements while preserving markdown content semantics. Automation assets will be organized by level: Playwright UI suites, PyTest API and integration suites, performance scripts, security scan configuration, and supporting fixtures. Repository ownership is shared between QA automation leads and engineering maintainers, with branch protections ensuring that test assets evolve together with application code.

Tracking updates are maintained through pull-request reviews, commit history, tagged releases, and linked Jira references. Every pull request that changes functionality in scope must evaluate whether test assets, traceability entries, or environment documentation need updating. Peer review is mandatory for repository changes affecting automation frameworks, baseline fixtures, network-shaping profiles, or evidence retention rules. CI/CD pipelines run repository-integrated validations on every pull request and on protected-branch merge events, publishing machine-readable artifacts to support historical comparison. This approach keeps the test repository as a governed quality asset rather than a side archive, making audits, rollback analysis, and regression prevention substantially stronger.

#### 4.3.3 Recording Pass/Fail Results
A test case is marked Pass only when the full expected outcome is met with supporting evidence and without contradictory telemetry in any dependent subsystem. For example, a successful avatar upload cannot be recorded as Pass if the UI updates correctly but gateway telemetry reveals route-policy mismatch, or if storage succeeds but malware scan timing cannot be proven to have occurred before persistence. Likewise, a negative test is Pass only when the system rejects the invalid condition in the required manner, such as returning 401 for expired sessions or blocking a spoofed file based on magic-number validation. Partial success does not qualify as Pass; it is either Fail or Blocked depending on root cause.

A test case is marked Fail when the actual result violates the acceptance criteria, design constraints, or mandated sequence controls. It is marked Blocked when execution cannot complete because of environment outage, unavailable telemetry, broken test data, or dependency failure unrelated to the specific product objective under evaluation. Automated results captured in CI/CD will be stored with immutable build IDs, while manual execution outcomes will be entered into the agreed test management tracker with links to screenshots, HAR/network captures, logs, and trace identifiers. Peer-review spot checks on pass/fail decisions will occur during high-risk execution cycles to ensure consistency of result interpretation across QA contributors. This standard is necessary because this story relies on distributed evidence, not single-screen verification.

### 4.4 Test Automation Strategy
The automation strategy is intentionally multi-tool because no single framework can adequately prove the breadth of controls in AD-488. Playwright is selected as the primary browser automation framework because it supports modern UI synchronization checks, network interception, robust element state validation, and deterministic browser-context management for valid, missing, and expired cookie sessions. It will validate hidden and disabled avatar controls, crop-flow access, dynamic DOM refresh behavior, absence of full page reload, and browser-side upload request characteristics. Playwright also enables instrumentation to inspect whether browser APIs associated with client-side image manipulation are invoked, helping close the identified self-healing gap related to proving raw binary transfer without compression or canvas processing.

PyTest is selected for API, service, and integration validation because it offers high maintainability for gateway checks, boundary-value data-driven testing, response-code verification, and service sequencing assertions. It will be used to validate dedicated gateway route traversal, route policy selection, 401 rejection behavior, 5MB boundary logic, MIME/magic-number verification, malware-positive rejection, storage-path placement, and telemetry correlation across the distributed flow. JMeter is selected for SLA-focused performance and concurrency-shaped traffic simulations, specifically to assess end-to-end timing under realistic load and payload boundary combinations. tcconfig is included to impose standard 4G network conditions consistently across performance and end-to-end executions. OWASP ZAP will be used in targeted security validation modes to inspect request/response handling, identify obvious upload-surface weaknesses, and validate that security headers and gateway behaviors are not regressed around the avatar endpoint.

Automation will be integrated into CI/CD in layers. Pull-request pipelines run fast feedback suites: unit/configuration checks, targeted Playwright smoke paths, PyTest API validations, and selected security probes. Nightly or scheduled QA pipelines run deeper integration, negative, and synchronization packs with richer telemetry capture. Pre-production pipelines execute controlled JMeter/tcconfig performance runs and final end-to-end synchronization checks before release review. All automation scripts require peer review before merge, coding standards for deterministic waits and assertions, reusable fixtures for token-state setup, and evidence publication on failure. The operational objective is not maximum automation count but dependable release evidence that can withstand audit and triage scrutiny.

#### 4.4.1 Automation Framework Mapping
Tool / Framework | Primary Purpose | Integration Strategy
---|---|---
Playwright | UI validation, auth gating, DOM synchronization, no-reload verification, browser upload inspection | Executed in pull-request smoke suites, nightly regression, and pre-prod end-to-end validation with screenshot, trace, and HAR retention
PyTest | API, gateway, service, integration, storage, CDN, and sequence validation | Executed in CI for fast API checks and in scheduled suites for deep integration with parametrized boundary and negative datasets
JMeter | SLA and transaction timing validation under controlled load | Executed in pre-prod and QA performance windows with exported timing dashboards and threshold assertions
tcconfig | Standardized 4G network throttling and adverse network shaping | Applied before JMeter and browser-driven SLA runs to enforce realistic transport conditions
OWASP ZAP | Targeted dynamic security checks for upload surface and endpoint hardening | Integrated as a controlled scan step in QA/pre-prod with reviewed findings and false-positive triage

### 4.5 Test Data Management

#### Data Requirements
Test data for AD-488 must represent both business-valid uploads and technically adversarial conditions. Required datasets include valid JPEG and WebP files below 5MB, exact-boundary 5.00MB files, 5.01MB oversize files, spoofed extension samples, invalid magic-number binaries, malware-positive samples or controlled scanner responses, expired-session requests, unauthenticated requests, and deterministic user accounts with profile visibility enabled. Because the story includes security and storage behavior, the data strategy must cover not just file fixtures but also session-state fixtures, gateway policy observability data, storage path expectations, CDN invalidation event identifiers, and browser-side baseline fingerprints for raw binary transfer comparison.

Data handling must comply with masking and safe-use principles. No real customer avatars or production identity data will be used. Synthetic user accounts and sanitized image fixtures will be generated and stored in controlled non-production repositories. Malware testing will use approved inert or laboratory-safe test signatures, or mock scanner responses where required, to avoid contamination risk in shared environments. Data seeding will be automated where possible so that CI/CD runs can recreate users, token states, and cleanup routines consistently. This reduces nondeterminism and ensures that failures reflect product behavior rather than stale data dependencies.

#### Data Availability
Test Type | Data Source | Environment | Data Type | Data Volume | Comments
---|---|---|---|---|---
Functional / Integration | Synthetic user profiles and image fixtures | Dev, QA, Pre-Prod | JPEG/WebP valid files | Moderate | Includes below-limit and exact-limit files
Negative / Validation | Security test fixture repository | QA, Pre-Prod | Spoofed extensions, invalid headers, malformed binaries | Moderate | Used for rejection and magic-number validation
Security | Approved malware simulation set / mock scanner | QA, Pre-Prod | Infected or flagged file surrogates | Low | Controlled handling required
Performance | Generated boundary-aligned payload set | QA, Pre-Prod | Timed transaction fixtures | Moderate | Used with 4G network shaping
UI State Synchronization | Synthetic user accounts with active sessions | QA, UAT, Pre-Prod | Session and profile state data | Low | Supports multi-view avatar validation
Gateway Controls | Gateway telemetry and route logs | QA, Pre-Prod | Route/policy metadata | Low | Required to prove dedicated path traversal

### 4.6 Test Environments
The testing landscape requires multiple controlled environments with aligned observability. Development environments support unit/configuration and early API checks, but primary confidence will be built in QA and Pre-Prod where gateway routing, malware scanning, storage bucket integration, CDN invalidation, and browser synchronization are all available. The QA environment must support controllable browser cookie session states, enabled inline malware scanning, storage audit visibility, CDN purge telemetry, and browser automation execution. Pre-Prod must mirror production-like routing and caching behavior closely enough to make SLA and propagation evidence credible. UAT will be used for business confirmation of user-visible behavior and operational readiness, not as the first point of technical discovery.

Environment configuration must include authorized cloud storage targets, dedicated test bucket folders, route and policy observability from the API Gateway, service logs for validation and malware scan sequencing, browser-compatible execution nodes, and tcconfig-capable hosts for standard 4G shaping. Time synchronization across environment components is mandatory so that event correlation remains trustworthy when proving the 2.0-second SLA and ordering guarantees. Environment drift checks should run before major execution cycles to confirm gateway rules, scanner availability, CDN purge hooks, and log retention settings are intact. Any environment lacking one of these capabilities may execute only a subset of tests and may not be used as sole evidence for release approval.

### 4.7 Entry and Exit Criteria

#### Entry Criteria
- Approved scope boundaries and validated test approach from upstream pipeline outputs
- Human-in-the-Loop Gate 2 architectural approval completed
- QA and Pre-Prod environments available with gateway, scanner, storage, and CDN telemetry enabled
- Synthetic test data and token-state fixtures prepared
- Automation frameworks configured in CI/CD
- Pull-request and branch-protection rules active

#### Exit Criteria
- All critical and high-priority test cases executed with no unresolved Critical defects
- No unresolved High defects against authentication gating, security validation, malware sequencing, storage placement, CDN invalidation, synchronization, or SLA compliance
- Traceability matrix reflects coverage for all structured requirements and review gaps
- Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 manual validation completed and pause state released by approver
- Release evidence package published with logs, traces, screenshots, performance metrics, and defect disposition

### 4.8 Test Reporting

#### 4.8.1 Status Reporting
Status reporting will occur daily during active QA cycles and at milestone checkpoints before UAT, Pre-Prod performance validation, and release recommendation. Reports will summarize executed versus planned tests, pass/fail/blocked counts, defect severity distribution, traceability completion, environment stability, risk trend movement, and any blockers to Gate 3 approval. Status reports should distinguish product failures from environment or observability failures so decision-makers can assess real release risk accurately.

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
Historical execution logs, screenshots, traces, HAR captures, timing reports, and linked observability exports will be retained in the build artifact repository and referenced from Jira and source control release notes. Retention must be long enough to support defect root-cause analysis, release audits, and rollback investigations. For this workstream, results should be preserved at least through the release cycle and one subsequent stabilization period. Test results are indexed by build number, branch, environment, and issue key so that QA and engineering can compare regressions over time rather than treating each run in isolation.

