## 6. Test Schedule / Milestone
A high-level schedule estimate for AD-488 assumes that architectural approval has already been granted and that integrated QA environment readiness can be confirmed rapidly. Test planning, traceability update, and data preparation require approximately 2 to 3 working days. Initial automation enhancement to close the identified review gaps—specifically browser raw-binary proof and gateway-route observability checks—requires approximately 3 to 5 working days depending on telemetry access. Core functional, negative, and integration automation plus manual exploratory support requires another 5 to 7 working days. Performance preparation with JMeter and tcconfig, plus SLA evidence collection under 4G shaping, requires 2 to 3 working days. UAT support, defect retest, and release evidence packaging require 2 to 4 working days. The total estimated effort window is approximately 3 weeks for a small cross-functional team, assuming stable environments and low defect churn.

Core resource alignment is as follows: one QA automation engineer at near-full allocation during automation build and regression hardening; one manual/exploratory QA analyst at partial-to-full allocation during integration and defect triage; one backend engineer and one frontend engineer at part-time allocation for instrumentation support and rapid bug fixes; one DevOps/platform engineer at part-time allocation for pipeline, environment, and network-shaping support; and one security engineer on-demand for targeted review of upload-surface findings. Milestones are: Day 1-3 planning and environment verification, Day 4-8 automation and integration build-out, Day 9-13 QA execution and defect triage, Day 14-16 performance and synchronization confirmation, Day 17-19 UAT/ops support and final regression, Day 20 Gate 3 pause/review and release recommendation.

## 7. Assumptions
- Gateway telemetry, route-policy logs, and malware scan traces are accessible in QA and Pre-Prod.
- Time synchronization across gateway, service, storage, CDN, and browser-visible reporting is sufficient for SLA correlation.
- Synthetic user accounts and safe image fixtures can be created without production dependencies.
- The crop experience in scope does not introduce unsupported client-side image-processing behavior beyond approved interactions.
- CI/CD runners can execute Playwright, PyTest, JMeter, OWASP ZAP, and tcconfig-supported workflows.
- Human approvers for Gate 2 and Gate 3 are available within planned milestone windows.
- Authorized cloud storage and CDN test hooks behave consistently enough in non-production to support reliable evidence collection.

## 8. Dependencies
- Stable authenticated web environment with controllable cookie-session states
- Dedicated API Gateway route and policy observability
- Validation service with magic-number inspection logging
- Inline malware scanning service with deterministic or mockable responses
- Authorized cloud storage bucket with audit visibility
- CDN invalidation telemetry and cache purge hooks
- CI/CD pipeline support for multi-tool execution
- Jira workflow availability for defects and approvals

