Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation Actions | Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Authentication token expiration or cookie-state inconsistency causes false positives or false negatives in UI gating and gateway 401 validation. | Medium | High | High | 1) Create deterministic valid, expired, and missing token fixtures. 2) Add Playwright session-state setup and teardown utilities. 3) Validate both UI state and gateway response in the same scenario. 4) Correlate browser state with gateway telemetry to detect environment drift. 5) Block release if auth-state reproducibility is unstable in QA or Pre-Prod. | QA Automation Engineer / Backend Engineer
R2 | Dedicated API Gateway route or filtering policy is misconfigured, causing requests to bypass intended controls or produce non-observable routing behavior. | Medium | High | High | 1) Add PyTest assertions against route identifiers and policy-selection logs. 2) Verify request path through gateway telemetry for accepted and rejected uploads. 3) Include gateway configuration review in pull-request checklist for impacted changes. 4) Execute pre-run environment drift check on routing rules. 5) Treat missing route observability as a release blocker because control efficacy cannot be proven. | Backend Engineer / DevOps Engineer
R3 | Malware scanning service latency or intermittent connection drops cause SLA breaches or non-deterministic persistence ordering. | Medium | High | High | 1) Instrument tracing spans across validation, scanner, and storage services. 2) Add PyTest sequence checks to prove no storage occurs before scan completion. 3) Use controlled malware responses for deterministic tests. 4) Isolate scanner latency in JMeter timing analysis. 5) Escalate if scanner instability threatens 2.0-second SLA or creates bypass ambiguity. | Backend Engineer / Security Engineer
R4 | CDN cache invalidation desynchronization delays avatar propagation across active views and header bars, creating stale user-visible identity artifacts. | Medium | High | High | 1) Capture storage-write and purge timestamps from telemetry. 2) Use Playwright multi-view validation to observe active-page propagation. 3) Add retry-aware diagnostics to distinguish UI lag from purge lag. 4) Include pre-prod cache behavior rehearsal before release sign-off. 5) Block release if stale avatars persist beyond accepted SLA window. | Frontend Engineer / DevOps Engineer
R5 | Browser or build changes introduce silent client-side compression, scaling, or canvas-based processing despite architecture constraints. | Low | High | Medium | 1) Add browser instrumentation to detect canvas/image processing API usage. 2) Capture and compare upload payload fingerprints against source fixtures. 3) Review front-end bundles for disallowed libraries or code paths in pull requests. 4) Include this check in regression suites for every UI-impacting change. 5) Escalate any deviation as an architectural non-compliance defect. | Frontend Engineer / QA Automation Engineer
R6 | Standard 4G simulation is inconsistently applied across environments, leading to unreliable SLA results and disputed release readiness. | Medium | Medium | Medium | 1) Standardize tcconfig profiles in source control. 2) Apply automated pre-checks to confirm network shaping is active before SLA runs. 3) Run JMeter and browser validation against the same shaping profile. 4) Record environmental metadata with every timing report. 5) Reject SLA evidence from runs lacking shaping verification. | DevOps Engineer / QA Automation Engineer

